														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2405434">
									

<p>I think I'm with Dr N. on this one. It's quite good, well-written and pleasantly devoid of unnecessary fluff, but it does feel as if a bit of polish would really bring out the shine&#8230;</p>
<p>Ideas/thoughts:</p>
<ul>
<li>Perhaps a bit more of a hint as to why they're there, why they're facing west, or what their connection to the forest/earth is.</li>
<li>Give the readers a hint as to what happened to the missing researcher; why did that one vanish when the other ones died? Even a vague justification would give the reader a better sense of what these things are and if they're inherently hostile, indifferent or beneficent (as long as nobody, y'know, hurts them).</li>
<li>Perhaps a reference to a local legend, an ancient myth or something like that would help.</li>
</ul>
<p>Now, I'm not saying &quot;everything needs a deep backstory with lots of supplements&quot;. Again, some of the best articles on the site are short-form. But, if you want to leave the reader with a creeping sense of discomfort or horror, a few pointedly unanswered questions are better than a huge, vague mystery without any points to really grab onto&#8230; Which is where we're currently at, I feel, and is why the good Dr. and I probably both feel that hard-to-define sense of &quot;good, but needs that extra somethin'&quot;.</p>
<p>Anyway, that's my two cents. Still quite good, regardless.</p>

